# User-authored instructions use the shared composer, without a bundled preset.
wait "role=button text=\"Open quick actions\"" 15000
press "role=button text=\"Open quick actions\""
wait "label=\"Edit shortcuts\"" 10000
press "label=\"Edit shortcuts\""
scroll down 3
wait "id=\"quick-command-name\"" 10000
# This field drops characters on iOS when typed at full speed -- a run saved
# "QA rview instructions" and the assertion on the real name then timed out.
# The tool's own guidance for a debounced field is to slow the keystrokes.
fill "id=\"quick-command-name\"" "QA review instructions" --delay-ms 60
# Filling the name raises the keyboard, which takes enough of the viewport to
# push the next field below the fold. `fill` refuses an off-screen target, so
# bring it back into view before reaching for it.
scroll down 1
wait "id=\"quick-command-value\"" 10000
fill "id=\"quick-command-value\"" "Review this project without changing any files" --delay-ms 60
# The iOS keyboard here exposes no dismiss key, and agent-device will not tap
# outside it to close one (no such tap can be proven side-effect-free). Its own
# guidance is to press the next target directly; the keyboard does not block a
# press the way it blocks a fill.
# The keyboard is still up, so the delivery tabs sit below the rendered
# window. Bring them into view before reaching for one.
scroll down 1
wait "id=\"quick-command-delivery-tab-collaboration\"" 10000
press "id=\"quick-command-delivery-tab-collaboration\""
press "id=\"quick-command-save\""
press "label=\"Done editing shortcuts\""
# Leaving edit mode already returns the saved command to the visible list.
# A viewport-length pull from the sheet header dismisses the native sheet.
wait "role=button label=\"QA review instructions\"" 10000
press "role=button label=\"QA review instructions\""
# A collaboration shortcut closes the sheet, opens the assistant strip over the
# terminal's own composer and leaves its instructions in the field. There is no
# second screen any more.
wait "id=\"assignment-bar-close\"" 10000
is visible "id=\"terminal-composer-input\" value=\"Review this project without changing any files\""
# A controlled replacement: the field is already filled, so the new value is
# verified before anything else is touched -- the runner enforces this.
fill "id=\"terminal-composer-input\"" "Keep this custom instruction draft while I inspect the terminal" --delay-ms 40
wait "id=\"terminal-composer-input\" value=\"Keep this custom instruction draft while I inspect the terminal\"" 10000
# The edit survives re-opening the shortcut, which is the property the screen
# this replaced also had -- checked on the device rather than assumed: a run
# that asserted the shortcut's template here found the reader's own words still
# in the field, which is the right answer and the one worth keeping.
is visible "id=\"terminal-composer-input\" value=\"Keep this custom instruction draft while I inspect the terminal\""
press "id=\"assignment-bar-close\""
# Re-open the same shortcut. The draft survives in the composer, which is now
# the only place a task is written -- this used to assert it survived in the
# task screen's own field, and that screen no longer exists.
press "role=button text=\"Open quick actions\""
press "role=button label=\"QA review instructions\""
wait "id=\"assignment-bar-close\"" 10000
# Re-opening the shortcut re-applies its text over whatever was in the field.
# A shortcut is a template, not a document that remembers -- the screen this
# replaced kept the reader's edit in a draft store, and that store went with it.
#
# Waited for rather than asserted outright, and the wait is what settles it: an
# immediate assertion races the write and catches the previous text still in
# place, which is how an earlier version of this line came to report one
# behaviour on one run and the opposite on the next.
wait "id=\"terminal-composer-input\" value=\"Review this project without changing any files\"" 10000
press "id=\"assignment-bar-close\""
# Delete the shortcut this flow created. The suite relaunches the app without
# erasing user data, and quick commands live in SecureStore -- which is the
# device keychain, so even uninstalling does not clear them. A run that leaves
# its fixture behind makes the next run's selector match two rows in distinct
# subtrees, which agent-device refuses to resolve. Cleaning up is the flow's
# own job.
press "role=button text=\"Open quick actions\""
wait "label=\"Edit shortcuts\"" 10000
press "label=\"Edit shortcuts\""
wait "label=\"Delete QA review instructions\"" 10000
press "label=\"Delete QA review instructions\""
is hidden "label=\"Delete QA review instructions\""
press "label=\"Done editing shortcuts\""
